This work examines a specific elliptic system defined by:
\begin{align*}
\begin{cases}
-\Delta u_i = f_i(u_i, u_j) & \text{in } \Omega, \\
u_i = 0 & \text{on } \partial\Omega,
\end{cases}
\end{align*}
where $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^N$ is a bounded domain with $N \geq 2$. Our analysis centers on the impact of the function $f_i$'s asymptotic behavior near zero on the existence of solutions. We establish that if $\lim_{t \to 0} f_i(t, s) = \lambda_i q_i |t|^{q_i-2}t$ for $1 < q_i < 2$, where $\lambda_i \in \mathbb{R}$ and $q_i$ satisfies specific conditions relative to the domain's first non-zero eigenvalue $\mu_1$, then solutions exist. Conversely, we demonstrate that if $f_i$ behaves like $\lambda_i \mu_1 |t|^{\mu_1-2}t$ as $t \to 0$, solutions do not exist.

We examine new physics contributions to the $b 	o u 	au  ar{
u}$ transition, utilizing recent experimental data from Belle II and LHCb. By analyzing ratios of branching fractions between the $b 	o u 	au  ar{
u}$ and $b 	o c 	au  ar{
u}$ processes, we derive constraints on these couplings. Our analysis indicates that the new physics couplings in $b 	o u 	au  ar{
u}$ are significantly weaker than those in $b 	o c 	au  ar{
u}$, a conclusion supported by current experimental inputs. Consequently, the potential for new physics to resolve the muon $g-2$ anomaly via these transitions remains limited.
